Prepare this host's context-independent controller prerequisites: the baseline host packages, installed through the host's own dnf, and the controller runtime, a venv on the OS Python holding exactly the wheels this build locks. Setup selects no context; the prerequisites a context adds are installed by the controller stage of apply. A ready host makes no request and no change. Otherwise the plan shows dnf's own preview before confirmation. --dry-run never elevates: it names the host package requirements without a preview and makes no changes, and --output json, accepted only with --dry-run, also lists every locked wheel's file, URL and SHA-256. --wheel-mirror reads each locked wheel as <mirror>/<filename> from a file:/// directory or an HTTPS base, as untrusted transport: the lock's SHA-256 alone decides whether the bytes are used. --download-only --dest-dir DIR never elevates and changes only DIR: on any connected host it writes the locked wheels of every Python ABI, the pip wheel and a copy of lock.json, so DIR serves an offline host as a --wheel-mirror. Review the plan before confirming; --yes skips ordinary confirmation.

Usage:
  bootwright setup [flags]

Examples:
  bootwright setup --dry-run

Flags:
      --dest-dir string       Write the locked wheels, the pip wheel and lock.json into this directory (requires --download-only)
      --download-only         Write the locked files for an offline host and change nothing else; never elevates
      --dry-run               Limit work to the command's dry-run boundary
      --output string         Select output mode (values: text, json) (default "text")
      --wheel-mirror string   Read each locked wheel as <mirror>/<filename>: file:///<directory> or https://<host>/<base>; its bytes must still match the lock
      --yes                   Confirm the command's ordinary confirmation

Global Flags:
      --context string             Ignored: setup selects no context
  -h, --help                       Show help for this command
      --ssh-ask-sudo-password      Request the borrowed account's sudo password
      --ssh-id-file string         Offer an explicit private key for eligible SSH access
      --ssh-user string            Borrow an explicit POSIX account for eligible SSH access
      --ssh-user-for-provisioned   Extend the borrowed account to provisioned machines
